  "slug": "ai-agents-biological-4390",
  "summary": "Four recent preprints apply agent orchestration to single-cell, variant, biosynthetic, and CRISPR guide workflows, making tool coordination a central concern.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is LLM or AI agents acting as orchestrators over established computational resources rather than as standalone predictors. Agent K uses an LLM-agent framework to coordinate bacterial genome analysis, biosynthetic gene cluster annotation, and pathway mapping toward candidate metabolites. Locally deployed open-weight agents coordinate a CRISPR guide-discovery pipeline starting from outbreak reports for diagnostic research. GENOAR applies a parallel, agent-assisted engine to preprocess and organize over 54 million human and mouse single-cell transcriptomes into analysis-ready form. BUDDY is a web platform integrating alignments, structure viewing, pathogenicity predictions, CRISPR reagent design, and AI-assisted design to model human missense variants via yeast orthologs, and it is less explicitly agentic than the others. What is new across these items is the scale of agent-mediated data organization and the use of locally deployed open-weight models for guide discovery.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-26"
}
